# Cursor/Codex/Claude/Copilot stakeholder-skill portability SOP

Every AEA stakeholder role must remain available to Cursor, Codex, Claude, and
Copilot with equivalent scope, authority, safety boundaries, routing, and
trigger intent. There are currently **10 roles**: `aea-project-manager`,
`aea-product-owner`, `aea-ux-designer`, `aea-customer-journey`,
`aea-support-coordinator`, `aea-ai-engineer`, `aea-devsecops-platform`,
`aea-senior-software-engineer`, `aea-mr-coordinator`, `aea-coherence-guardian`.

Gemini and Grok are **not yet covered**. Their repo-level skill/instruction
conventions were not confirmed against real documentation when Copilot was
added — do not add adapters for them by guessing the format; confirm the
convention first (see "Adding a new target tool" below).

## Source and equivalent

- `.cursor/skills/aea-*/` is the canonical role definition and owns companion
  references such as `walk.md`, `routing.md`, or `posture.md`.
- `.agents/skills/aea-*/SKILL.md` is the generated Codex discovery adapter.
- `.agents/skills/aea-*/agents/openai.yaml` is Codex UI/invocation metadata.
- `.claude/skills/aea-*/SKILL.md` is the generated Claude discovery adapter.
- `.github/instructions/aea-*.instructions.md` is the generated Copilot
  discovery adapter (`applyTo: '**'` — Copilot has no persona-switch, so
  relevance is decided by the canonical skill's own triggers, applied at the
  user's or Copilot's discretion, not by file-glob matching).
- `.github/copilot-instructions.md` is the repo-wide Copilot context file
  (hand-authored, analogous to `CLAUDE.md`) that points at the per-role
  instructions.
- Codex, Claude, and Copilot adapters reference the canonical role at
  runtime; do not copy the full role body into `.agents`, `.claude`, or
  `.github/instructions`.

## Mandatory same-change workflow

When a role update starts on any side:

1. Apply the semantic role change to the canonical `.cursor` skill.
2. Update the matching entry in
   `scripts/generate_codex_stakeholder_skills.py` when its trigger,
   adaptation, display behavior, or linked-reference instruction changes.
   Avoid an unescaped `: ` (colon-space) inside the single-line `description`
   string passed to `SKILLS` — the generated adapters render frontmatter as
   `description: <text>` on one line, not the canonical skill's `>-` folded
   block, and a bare colon there breaks frontmatter parsing (this happened
   with the first `aea-coherence-guardian` description and showed up as the
   Skill listing displaying the generated HTML comment instead of the real
   description). Use an em dash or restructure the sentence instead.
3. Regenerate all adapters:

   `python scripts/generate_codex_stakeholder_skills.py`

4. Update the matching `agents/openai.yaml` when display name, short
   description, default prompt, dependency, or implicit-invocation policy
   changes. The generator does not create this file — author it by hand
   for any new role, matching the format of existing `openai.yaml` files.
5. Validate the equivalent set:

   `python scripts/generate_codex_stakeholder_skills.py --check`

6. Run the official Codex skill validator for every affected `.agents` skill and
   validate every affected Claude and Copilot adapter's frontmatter and
   generated content.
7. Commit Cursor, Codex, Claude, and Copilot changes in the same issue,
   branch, and MR.

Do not hand-edit a generated Codex, Claude, or Copilot file as the final
change. Translate a tool-side proposal into the canonical role first,
regenerate, then review each tool-specific adaptation. Do not merge a
one-sided role change.

## Adding or removing a role

Any new or removed `aea-*` stakeholder must be added or removed in all four
representations, including Codex metadata and the generator inventory. The
synchronization check fails on inventory mismatch, canonical digest drift,
missing adapters, or missing `openai.yaml` metadata
(`python scripts/generate_codex_stakeholder_skills.py --check`).

A role that only exists for one tool (e.g. was prototyped Claude-only before
this SOP existed) is **not** equivalent and must be promoted to canonical
`.cursor/skills/` status — written in Cursor idiom (canvas references,
`@mention` routing) — before it counts as a real team member. This happened
for `aea-coherence-guardian`: it started as a Claude-only addition, then was
promoted to `.cursor/skills/aea-coherence-guardian/` and added to the
generator so Codex and Claude both get a real adapter.

## Equivalence boundary

Equivalent means the same role purpose, authority, exclusions, safety rules,
handoffs, and delivery SOP. Tool-surface translations are expected: Cursor
browser/canvas/Cloud Agent instructions become the approved Codex or Claude
browser, visualization/design, and delegation rules. Equivalence does not require
byte-identical files.

## Claude Code mechanic translations

Claude Code has no `.cursor/skills/` scanner, no chat `@mention`
agent-invocation, and no `.canvas.tsx` renderer, so the generated Claude
adapter's "Claude adaptations" section plus this table are what make a
Cursor-authored role actually usable in Claude Code:

| Cursor mechanic | Claude Code translation |
|---|---|
| `@aea-<role>` mention routing | `Skill({skill: "aea-<role>"})` to continue as that persona in the current turn, or `Agent({subagent_type: "general-purpose", prompt: "..."})` briefed with the role's canonical `.cursor/skills/aea-<role>/SKILL.md` path for independent/parallel work |
| `~/.cursor/skills-cursor/canvas/SKILL.md` → `.canvas.tsx` | Load the `artifact-design` skill and publish with the `Artifact` tool (HTML) |
| `cursor-ide-browser` MCP (`browser_tabs`, `browser_navigate`, `browser_lock`, `browser_snapshot`) | `mcp__Claude_Browser__*` tools: `tabs_context`, `navigate`, `computer`, `read_page` (no `browser_lock` equivalent — nothing else shares that session's browser pane) |
| Cursor Cloud Agents | Claude Code background `Agent` execution, optionally `isolation: "worktree"` or `"remote"` |
| `disable-model-invocation: true` | No Claude Code frontmatter equivalent — the canonical role's own text states when it should only be explicitly invoked; honor it by convention, same as every other hard constraint |
| `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` with `alwaysApply: true` | Not auto-injected in Claude Code — `Read` the referenced `.mdc` file at the point of use instead of assuming prior injection |

Codex has its own equivalents (installed browser/visualization skills,
`$aea-<role>` invocation, Codex task/thread delegation policy) captured
directly in each Codex adapter's "Codex adaptations" section — this table is
Claude-specific because Claude Code's adaptation notes live in one shared
place (this SOP) rather than being fully rebuilt per role in
`scripts/generate_codex_stakeholder_skills.py`. Copilot's own translation
(no persona-switch, `applyTo: '**'`, explicit-ask activation) lives in each
generated `.github/instructions/aea-*.instructions.md` file's "Copilot
adaptations" section.

## Adding a new target tool (e.g. Gemini, Grok)

Before adding a fifth representation, confirm the tool's actual repo-level
skill or instruction-file convention against real documentation or a working
example — do not invent a plausible-looking format. A fabricated convention
produces files that look authoritative but do not actually integrate with
that tool, which is worse than not adding it. Once confirmed:

1. Add a `<TOOL>_TARGET` path and a `render_<tool>` function to
   `scripts/generate_codex_stakeholder_skills.py`, following the existing
   `render_codex` / `render_claude` / `render_copilot` pattern (thin pointer
   to the canonical `.cursor/skills/{name}/SKILL.md`, tool-specific
   adaptations section, canonical-sha256 comment if the format supports
   comments).
2. Wire it into `main()`'s per-role loop and inventory-mismatch check, same
   as the three existing targets.
3. Update this SOP's frontmatter `globs`, the role/tool count above, and add
   a row set (or its own translation table) documenting that tool's
   mechanic differences from Cursor.
4. Regenerate, `--check`, and commit alongside the other four
   representations per the same-change workflow above.
